      "text": "Let $F$ be the focus of the ellipse, and let $p$ be the distance between the foci. \n\nWe can use the geometric facts mentioned by \"Curiosity\" to solve this problem. We know that the distance between the foci is the sum of the semi-major and semi-minor axes, since the foci lie on the major axis. Thus, $p = 6$.\n\nApplying Ptolemy's theorem to the right and left triangles formed by the radii drawn from the center of the ellipse to the foci, we can use the relationship that $a^2 = b^2 + c^2$ from the Pythagorean theorem, where $a$ is the semi-major axis and $b$ is the semi-minor axis, and $c$ is the distance from the center to a focus. We know that $a = 5$ and $b = 4$, so $c = 3$. \n\nNow, the radius of the circle can be computed by the difference of the distances from the center of the ellipse to the foci of the circle. This is because the circle is tangent to the ellipse. Thus, the radius of the circle is equal to $F - p = 3 - 6 = -3$. Since the radius cannot be negative, we take the absolute value.\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is $\\boxed{3}$.\n```python\n# Calculation of the radius of the circle\n# Given values\na = 5  # Semi-major axis\nb = 4  # Semi-minor axis\np = a + b  # Distance between the foci\n\n# Radius of the circle\nradius = abs(p - a)\nprint(radius)\n```",
